WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.200
In 1665, Robert Hooke saw rows of tiny boxes in a thin slice of cork.

2
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.640
He called the boxes cells.

3
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.900
What decides what a microscope can show?

4
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.140
And why was seeing those boxes not enough?

5
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.400
We start with the tool.

6
00:00:21.483 --> 00:00:23.043
Two words matter here.

7
00:00:24.423 --> 00:00:28.943
Magnification is how many times larger an image looks than the real object.

8
00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:35.203
Resolution is the smallest distance between two points at which you still see two

9
00:00:35.203 --> 00:00:36.203
separate points.

10
00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:40.773
Watch two dots on a slide under an ordinary light microscope.

11
00:00:42.013 --> 00:00:44.873
A micrometer is one thousandth of a millimeter.

12
00:00:46.093 --> 00:00:48.913
The dots start close together and look like one blur.

13
00:00:50.413 --> 00:00:51.453
Slowly they move apart.

14
00:00:52.773 --> 00:00:56.953
Near a gap of about 0.2 micrometers, they begin to look like two.

15
00:00:58.017 --> 00:01:04.597
Why the blur? Even a well-made ordinary light microscope blurs the finest details.

16
00:01:06.077 --> 00:01:08.977
Light spreads out as it passes through the opening of the lens.

17
00:01:10.397 --> 00:01:14.417
So each point becomes a small smudge, and close smudges merge into one.

18
00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:19.230
Now set the dots 0.15 micrometers apart.

19
00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:21.590
They look like one blur.

20
00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.190
We triple the magnification.

21
00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:27.210
Will you see one dot or two?

22
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:38.187
Still one. The blur got bigger, and it is still one blur.

23
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:42.067
Making the image bigger does not shrink the limit.

24
00:01:43.607 --> 00:01:45.587
Magnification makes an image bigger.

25
00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:49.447
Resolution decides how much detail it holds.

26
00:01:50.517 --> 00:01:51.897
So here is the rule.

27
00:01:53.197 --> 00:01:57.577
A tool shows a detail only if the detail is larger than the tool's limit.

28
00:01:58.977 --> 00:02:00.737
Enlarging the image adds no detail.

29
00:02:01.977 --> 00:02:04.517
A whole cell can be clear while its parts are not.

30
00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:07.350
Each tool has its own limit.

31
00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:12.690
Picture a ladder of sizes, each rung ten times smaller than the one above.

32
00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.710
Your eye's limit sits near a tenth of a millimeter.

33
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.570
The light microscope's gold line sits far lower.

34
00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:24.370
The electron microscope's line sits lower still.

35
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:30.420
Hooke needed a microscope to make out the outlines and spacing of the cork boxes.

36
00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:37.360
Much smaller or low-contrast details inside cells needed better resolution, more

37
00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.040
contrast and later tools.

38
00:02:40.183 --> 00:02:41.543
But look at what he had.

39
00:02:42.823 --> 00:02:45.583
Empty walls, left after the cork's cells had died.

40
00:02:46.743 --> 00:02:48.903
One material, from one kind of plant.

41
00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:52.943
That cannot show that all living things are made of cells.

42
00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:57.827
Making out cells needs a tool with fine enough resolution.

43
00:02:59.207 --> 00:03:04.007
Building the theory needed more: comparing many kinds of living things, and testing

44
00:03:04.007 --> 00:03:05.427
where new cells come from.

45
00:03:06.647 --> 00:03:13.067
About 175 years passed before scientists argued that plants and animals alike are

46
00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:13.867
built of cells.
